                    },
                    {
                        "form_id": "8b3b78f7-54e0-4a03-a0b9-7572ef1764b2",
                        "entry_id": "a4a6fe6d-02f1-3e9f-8790-300817be81b0",
                        "tags": [
                            "plural",
                            "prepositional"
                        ],
                        "form": "ба́снях"
                    }
                ],
                "senses": [
                    {
                        "sense_id": "7c090096-0c05-3267-8d08-7807087f3cb3",
                        "entry_id": "a4a6fe6d-02f1-3e9f-8790-300817be81b0",
                        "glosses": [
                            "короткий аллегорический рассказ или стихотворение, как правило — с нравоучительным заключением"
                        ],
                        "tags": [],
                        "examples": [
                            {
                                "example_id": "0d9565dd-b6d7-4605-ae2b-b7e5c96766e5",
                                "sense_id": "7c090096-0c05-3267-8d08-7807087f3cb3",
                                "text": "Как всякая поэзия, и басня говорит образами: она рисует и осла, и лисицу, и льва, и соловья; первый у неё добродушно глуп, вторая увёртливо хитра, третий грозно могущ .."
                            }
                        ]
                    },
                    {
                        "sense_id": "159e48ed-5184-388b-83c9-8ed0e0814600",
                        "entry_id": "a4a6fe6d-02f1-3e9f-8790-300817be81b0",
                        "glosses": [
                            "перен. выдумка, вымысел, враньё"
                        ],
                        "tags": [],
                        "examples": [
                            {
                                "example_id": "1f63d03a-57aa-4c8c-876b-64b18022a7f7",
                                "sense_id": "159e48ed-5184-388b-83c9-8ed0e0814600",
                                "text": "Всё это не более как преданье и басни в угоду слабоумных и малограмотных."
                            }
                        ]
                    }
                ],
                "translations": [
                    {
                        "translation_id": "5b21a504-0815-40c9-87a2-3341844e1473",
                        "source_entry_id": "a4a6fe6d-02f1-3e9f-8790-300817be81b0",
                        "target_lang_word": "fable",
                        "target_lang_code": "en",
                        "target_sense_ids": [],
                        "cross_references": [
                            {
                                "target_word": "fable",
                                "target_lang_code": "en",
                                "target_senses": [
                                    {
                                        "sense_id": "479b578b-22d3-3c0d-a4ff-a4a38ac6ed54",
                                        "entry_id": "86a87c18-b728-37ff-9e29-150f26e4a533",
                                        "glosses": [
                                            "A fictitious narrative intended to enforce some useful truth or precept, usually with animals, etc. as characters; an apologue. Prototypically, Aesop's Fables."
                                        ],
                                        "tags": [],
                                        "examples": []
                                    },
                                    {
                                        "sense_id": "69ec85de-193b-31eb-9e97-ba05d7297338",
                                        "entry_id": "86a87c18-b728-37ff-9e29-150f26e4a533",
                                        "glosses": [
                                            "Any story told to excite wonder; common talk; the theme of talk."
                                        ],
                                        "tags": [],
                                        "examples": [
                                            {
                                                "example_id": "e90778b3-447c-4e52-868e-72124e54da46",
                                                "sense_id": "69ec85de-193b-31eb-9e97-ba05d7297338",
                                                "text": "But refuſe prophane and olde wiues fables, and exerciſe thy ſelfe rather vnto godlineſſe."
                                            },
                                            {
                                                "example_id": "c1945966-2461-4cc0-8da2-63a59023cf09",
                                                "sense_id": "69ec85de-193b-31eb-9e97-ba05d7297338",
                                                "text": "[…]we grew / The fable of the city where we dwelt."
                                            }
                                        ]
                                    },
                                    {
                                        "sense_id": "c8ed8240-ea65-3a86-b68d-adffdc69c964",
                                        "entry_id": "86a87c18-b728-37ff-9e29-150f26e4a533",
                                        "glosses": [
                                            "Fiction; untruth; falsehood."
                                        ],
                                        "tags": [],
                                        "examples": [
                                            {
                                                "example_id": "51618d27-7e30-48b0-bef1-28d6e968f61f",
                                                "sense_id": "c8ed8240-ea65-3a86-b68d-adffdc69c964",
                                                "text": "I say it would look like a fable to report that this gentleman gives away all which is the overplus of a great fortune by secret methods to other men."
                                            }
                                        ]
                                    },
                                    {
                                        "sense_id": "ef5ffd3e-b99b-3200-9b9a-adebad754bd1",
                                        "entry_id": "86a87c18-b728-37ff-9e29-150f26e4a533",
                                        "glosses": [
                                            "The plot, story, or connected series of events forming the subject of an epic or dramatic poem."
                                        ],
                                        "tags": [],
                                        "examples": [
                                            {
                                                "example_id": "3e43b821-d3db-4c80-a5eb-02a0dd515ca3",
                                                "sense_id": "ef5ffd3e-b99b-3200-9b9a-adebad754bd1",
                                                "text": "For the moral (as Bossu observes,) is the first business of the poet, as being the groundwork of his instruction. This being formed, he contrives such a design, or fable, as may be most suitable to the moral;"
                                            }
                                        ]
                                    }
                                ]
                            },
                            {
                                "target_word": "fable",
                                "target_lang_code": "en",
                                "target_senses": [
                                    {
                                        "sense_id": "3c42f002-c690-347d-8e7c-3a034858c1f0",
                                        "entry_id": "cd1ed384-1685-3424-bbf1-4abe12e6021b",
                                        "glosses": [
                                            "To compose fables; hence, to write or speak fiction; to write or utter what is not true."
                                        ],
                                        "tags": [
                                            "archaic",
                                            "intransitive"
                                        ],
                                        "examples": [
                                            {
                                                "example_id": "99f15d6c-53c7-4664-b5b7-036a3a835dc7",
                                                "sense_id": "3c42f002-c690-347d-8e7c-3a034858c1f0",
                                                "text": "He Fables not, I heare the enemie: / Out ſome light Horſemen, and peruſe their Wings."
                                            },
                                            {
                                                "example_id": "54645737-5de3-494b-9c22-3e3f64c6d8fc",
                                                "sense_id": "3c42f002-c690-347d-8e7c-3a034858c1f0",
                                                "text": "Vain now the Tales which fab’ling Poets tell, / That wav’ring Conqueſt ſtill deſires to rove; / In Marlbrô’s Camp the Goddeſs knows to dwell: / Long as the Hero’s Life remains her Love."
                                            },
                                            {
                                                "example_id": "e82f8876-cbfa-4675-9543-349276f7d7fc",
                                                "sense_id": "3c42f002-c690-347d-8e7c-3a034858c1f0",
                                                "text": "1852, Matthew Arnold, Empedocles on Etna, Act II, in Empedocles on Etna and Other Poems, London: B. Fellowes, p. 50,\nHe fables, yet speaks truth."
                                            }
                                        ]
                                    },
                                    {
                                        "sense_id": "fc75df17-9370-34a4-9c1d-6cbea1cb1519",
                                        "entry_id": "cd1ed384-1685-3424-bbf1-4abe12e6021b",
                                        "glosses": [
                                            "To make up; to devise, and speak of, as true or real; to tell of falsely; to recount in the form of a fable."
                                        ],
                                        "tags": [
                                            "archaic",
                                            "transitive"
                                        ],
                                        "examples": [
                                            {
                                                "example_id": "a527cc7e-11ef-4766-97d3-9b8cbc9512ac",
                                                "sense_id": "fc75df17-9370-34a4-9c1d-6cbea1cb1519",
                                                "text": "[…] erre not that ſo ſhall end / The ſtrife of Glorie: which we mean to win, / Or turn this Heav’n itſelf into the Hell / Thou fableſt […]"
                                            },
                                            {
                                                "example_id": "afd09679-4256-4079-96b2-08fae8c91345",
                                                "sense_id": "fc75df17-9370-34a4-9c1d-6cbea1cb1519",
                                                "text": "THE Poets Fable, That Apollo being enamoured of Caſſandra, was by her many ſhifts and cunning ſlights ſtill deluded in his Deſire […]"
                                            },
                                            {
                                                "example_id": "ee1774bd-f872-440f-bb3b-d2ebb9f16b29",
                                                "sense_id": "fc75df17-9370-34a4-9c1d-6cbea1cb1519",
                                                "text": "Fabled by the daughters of memory. And yet it was in some way if not as memory fabled it. A phrase, then, of impatience, thud of Blake’s wings of excess."
                                            }
                                        ]
                                    }
                                ]
                            }
                        ]
                    }
                ],
                "word_linkages": [
